No, Timothy. Given the context, only two options are available: 1) he's
still here, or 2) that he's gone.
I interpreted it to mean "He has left", which is unambiguous, but it could
have meant "He is left", i.e., he remains (is still here).
My point remains. It's a bad example, albeit for slightly different reasons
than I originally thought.

stevo

